From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Mon, 8 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] aperture: Add a camera control API

Aperture has no notion of a camera control today -- the viewfinder
documentation says as much: "The viewfinder does not contain any camera
controls, these must be implemented yourself." That leaves applications
with no way to offer manual focus, manual exposure or white balance,
which is what snapshot#215 asks for.

GStreamer is not much help here: pipewiresrc streams from a PipeWire node
but does not expose that node parameters, so controls cannot be reached
through the pipeline at all. Patching pipewiresrc to proxy params would
benefit every PipeWire client, but it adds gst-plugins-bad as a third
upstream for what is otherwise a self-contained change. Instead bind the
node directly, using the id the device provider already hands us in the
GstDevice property "object.id".

Controls are discovered from SPA_PARAM_PropInfo and written with
SPA_PARAM_Props, which gives a generic control list for free: PipeWire
maps its six well-known properties onto SPA ids and everything else onto
SPA_PROP_START_CUSTOM + <driver control id>, so any control a driver
exposes is addressable, and enumerated controls arrive complete with
their value labels. A libcamera camera with an autofocus lens therefore
turns up as

    AfTrigger      enum [AfTriggerStart=0, AfTriggerCancel=1]
    AfMode         enum [AfModeManual=0, AfModeContinuous=2]
    LensPosition   0..10 default 1

with no libcamera-specific code in aperture. Controls are matched by name
rather than by id, because ids outside PipeWire well-known set are only
stable for a given driver version.

Two properties of the underlying interface shape the API and are worth
being explicit about:

  - Props is declared write-only, so there is no read-back and no change
    notification. A caller owns the values it sets, and should seed its
    UI from CameraControl::default_value() rather than from the device.

  - Controls are silently discarded while the camera is idle: the
    libcamera plugin queues them and only merges them into a Request in
    spa_libcamera_buffer_recycle(), under "if (impl->active)". Callers
    have to wait until the viewfinder is Ready. This is documented on
    set_control() because it otherwise looks exactly like a permission
    failure.

supported_controls() returns an empty list when a camera exposes nothing
adjustable or when the node cannot be reached, so a UI built on it hides
itself on hardware that cannot be adjusted rather than offering a slider
that moves nothing. The PipeWire connection is opened lazily on first
use, since most cameras are never asked for their controls.

Tested on a Samsung Galaxy S9 (SDM845, IMX345 with an AK7375 focus
actuator) running libcamera 0.7.2 and PipeWire 1.6.8, reading the
actuator back over V4L2 to confirm the lens really moves. The front
camera, which is fixed-focus, correctly reports no focus controls. See
aperture/examples/controls.rs.

